<DocLayout title="API reference" eyebrow="Developer first" description="The small public surface for creating a matcher, applying updates, inspecting state, and asserting the invariant." next={{ label: "Run examples", href: "/examples/" }}>
  <h2>Constructor</h2>
  <pre><code>{`Matcher(n: int, mode: "basic" | "multilevel")`}</code></pre>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Argument</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>Number of vertices. Vertices are integers in <code>[0, n)</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mode</code></td><td>Exactly <code>basic</code> or <code>multilevel</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>insert(u, v)</code></td><td>Adds an existing or new graph edge and repairs the matching.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>delete(u, v)</code></td><td>Removes an existing graph edge and repairs any affected matching state.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Inspection</h2>
  <pre><code>{`matcher.matching()  # matching edge snapshot
matcher.size()      # number of matching edges
matcher.maximal()   # invariant check
matcher.stats()     # update and rebuild ledger`}</code></pre>
  <p>Inspection methods are read-only from the caller’s perspective. Treat returned collections as snapshots and continue to mutate the graph through <code>insert</code> and <code>delete</code>.</p>

  <h2>Validation and failure</h2>
  <p>Invalid vertices, self-loops, malformed edges, unsupported modes, and inconsistent deletions are rejected explicitly. A failed update must not expose a partially applied graph or matching state.</p>
  <div class="doc-callout"><p>For reproducible experiments, record the mode, vertex count, update sequence, and <code>stats()</code> ledger alongside your result.</p></div>
</DocLayout>
